[
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Data</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Int</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Notation</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- attribute [-notation] ?</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">my_definition</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- doesn't work</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Any way to disable notation (even locally)?</p>",
        "id": 579516564,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1773555559
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I am using blackboard bold for variables and would like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">𝕏</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕐</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">T</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 579516706,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1773555664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not an answer to the question, but don't you think that using standard notation to mean something else could lead to confusion when people read your code?</p>\n<p>Did you consider usual bold letters instead?</p>",
        "id": 579536973,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773571400
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Disable.20notation.20locally.3F/near/579536973\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Not an answer to the question, but don't you think that using standard notation to mean something else could lead to confusion when people read your code?</p>\n<p>Did you consider usual bold letters instead?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Maybe <code>def ℤ</code> is misleading: I only use these letters to talk about objects of a category, e.g.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">interchange</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">𝕏</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕐</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕎</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Change</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕏</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕐</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕫</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕎</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≅</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕏</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕫</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">𝕐</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊗</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">𝕎</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>The text that I'm formalising uses blackboard bold, which is why I chose to use them</p>",
        "id": 579557166,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Chen",
        "timestamp": 1773589610
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Disable.20notation.20locally.3F/near/579536973\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Not an answer to the question, but don't you think that using standard notation to mean something else could lead to confusion when people read your code?<br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>There are many valid reasons to override standard notation. For instance rewriting the underlying theory for reasons ranging from “I want this particular piece of math to work this particular way” to “I’d like my students to learn math or good api design by writing their own Int api from scratch”</p>",
        "id": 579570599,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1773603553
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, teaching is a rather common reason for that.</p>",
        "id": 579574455,
        "sender_full_name": "Patrick Massot",
        "timestamp": 1773608213
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Indeed; in NNG4 we have</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">notation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">name</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyNatNotation</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">priority</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1000000</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"ℕ\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">MyNat</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>but that is not a solution here (and to be honest I don't know if this even works; I am not sure whether we import <code>Mathlib.Init.Data.Nat.Notation</code> in NNG and I am not sure I know how to find out...</p>",
        "id": 579575205,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Buzzard",
        "timestamp": 1773608933
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can write the very hacky:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Parser</span>\n<span class=\"n\">run_meta</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">modifyEnv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">parserExtension</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">modifyState</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">with</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">tokens</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">tokens</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">values</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">erase</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"ℤ\"</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">foldl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">acc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">acc</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">insert</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{}</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">})</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">check</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- unknown identifier</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>which basically just regenerates the token table without <code>ℤ</code>. I'm pretty sure this only works within the same file though.</p>",
        "id": 579577050,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1773610703
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"110038\">Kevin Buzzard</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/270676-lean4/topic/Disable.20notation.20locally.3F/near/579575205\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am not sure whether we import <code>Mathlib.Init.Data.Nat.Notation</code> in NNG and I am not sure I know how to find out...</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><code>lake exe graph ./import_graph.pdf --include-deps --from Mathlib.Data.Nat.Notation</code>, you do import it</p>",
